request.description:
Search request object with "search_mode" ("fast" or "smart") and a "query" object.
## SMART MODE Use smart mode when the user's request is best expressed as a natural-language information need and the search system should infer entities, dates, source/category intent, and content types. Do not call find_companies first just for smart search; company names in text are resolved internally.
### Query object structure: { "text": "...", "context": "...", "max_chunks": 20 } - text: the actual information need. Include entity, topic/aspect, and time period, when applicable. Use natural language, not keyword bags. - context (optional): brief natural-language hints that help smart search narrow or disambiguate. Use for scope/source/content hints such as "search in my private files", "focus on transcripts and filings", "search in Financial Times", or "prioritize broker reports". - max_chunks (default 20): Increase only if the same query needs more evidence; otherwise make a separate focused query.
### Smart mode example: {
  "request": {
    "search_mode": "smart",
    "query": { "text": "Apple's iPhone revenue discussion in Q1 2025 earnings call"}
  }
}
### Smart mode with context example: {
  "request": {
    "search_mode": "smart",
    "query": { 
      "text": "Tesla battery technology developments",
      "context": "focus on transcripts and broker reports"
    }
  }
}
### Smart mode Best practices: - Write full natural language requests that explain what you're looking for. Avoid pure keyword-style queries. - Include the entity name, topic or aspect you care about, time period (Q1 2024, last month, etc.) and content type hints (10-K, earnings call, news, broker report, etc.).  - Use "context" when you need to provide additional hints about where to search or how to narrow down the results. --
## FAST MODE Use fast mode when you can improve retrieval by applying explicit filters manually, such as a known entity ID or exact keyword. If you are unsure whether manual filters help, use smart mode.
### Query object structure: { "text": "...", "max_chunks": 20, "filters": { ... } } - text: natural language search string - always include it unless doing a very broad search where any of these filters are populated: entity, keyword, reporting_entities or tags. - max_chunks (default 20): Increase only if the same query needs more evidence; otherwise make a separate focused query. - filters: manual filters you choose to narrow results.
### Filters object structure (each supports any_of / all_of / none_of): - entity: entity IDs (resolve via find_securities first). - keyword: keywords for exact string matching (min 3 chars each). - timestamp: { "start": "...", "end": "..." } in ISO 8601. - category: content types (e.g. "filings", "news", "research", "transcripts", "my_files"). - reporting_entities: reporting entity IDs for filings and earnings calls. - reporting_periods: fiscal year/quarter for filings and earnings calls. - sentiment: the text chunk forecasted market impact ("positive", "negative", "neutral") - document_type: "FILING", "INVESTMENT-RESEARCH", "NEWS", "TRANSCRIPT", "TRANSCRIPT-PRESENTATION" - tag: custom tags associated to proprietary uploaded documents (min 3 chars each).
### Fast mode example: {
  "request": {
    "search_mode": "fast",
    "query": {
      "text": "Impact of semiconductor supply chain disruptions to Nvidia's manufacturing capacity",
      "filters": {
        "entity": {
          "search_in": "ALL",
          "any_of": [
            "E09E2B"
          ]
        },
        "keyword": {
          "search_in": "ALL",
          "any_of": [
            "semiconductor"
          ]
        }
      },
      "max_chunks": 20
    }
  }
}

